@meta
id: 16-17
title: Tabs are layouts
chapter: 16
type: lesson
skills: tabpages
par: 130
minutes: 6
vscode: a tab is a file; here it is not
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
Vim has tabs, and the word is a trap. A VS Code tab is a file. A vim
tab is a page: a whole arrangement of windows, with its own splits and
sizes, that you can switch to and back. The buffer list is shared
across every tab. Open a file in tab 2 and it is in `:ls` from tab 1
as well, because the tab was never holding the file; it was holding a
layout.

`:tabnew errors.json` opens a new tab page with one window showing
that file. `gt` goes to the next tab, `gT` to the previous, and both
wrap round. `:tabclose` closes the current page; its buffers stay
loaded. `:tabs` lists the pages and what each one is showing.

The refund window is being shortened and the error table is missing
its code. Do the errors file in its own tab, then come back.

  1. `errors.json`: directly under `ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED`, add

         "REFUND_WINDOW_EXPIRED": { "statusCode": 409, "sentry": false },

  2. `order.provider.ts`: `days <= 30` becomes `days <= 14`.

`:wqa` writes every buffer in every tab and closes all of it.

@keys
| `:tabnew name` | a new tab page showing `name`; `:tabnew` alone shows an empty buffer |
| `gt` `gT` | next tab page, previous tab page; `2gt` goes to page 2 |
| `:tabclose` | close this page; `:tabonly` closes every other one |
| `:tabs` | list the pages and their windows |
| `:wqa` `:qa` | across every tab, not just this one |

@hints
`:tabnew errors.json`, `/SHIPPED`, `o`, the new entry, `Esc`.
--
`gt` from the last page wraps to the first, so one `gt` is back to the
provider. `:tabs` if you want to see the two pages listed.
--
`/<= 30`, `f3`, `cw14`, `Esc`, `:wqa`.

@solution
    :tabnew errors.json<Enter>  /SHIPPED<Enter>  o "REFUND_WINDOW_EXPIRED": { "statusCode": 409, "sentry": false }, <Esc>
    gt  /<= 30<Enter>  f3  cw 14 <Esc>  :wqa<Enter>

Around a hundred and fifteen keystrokes, most of them the JSON line;
the tab cost the same as a split would have.

So what is a tab for, if not a file? A second layout. One page with the
provider and its test side by side, another with the migration and the
schema, and `gt` between the two jobs without disturbing either
arrangement. That is a real use, and it comes up perhaps once a day.
The next lesson is about the use that comes up every minute in VS Code
and is the wrong tool here.

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    const days = (Date.now() - order.placedAt.getTime()) / 86_400_000;

    if (days <= 30) {
      await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

      return this.orderRepository.update({
        where: { id },
        values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
      });
    }

    throw exception('REFUND_WINDOW_EXPIRED');
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    const days = (Date.now() - order.placedAt.getTime()) / 86_400_000;

    if (days <= 14) {
      await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

      return this.orderRepository.update({
        where: { id },
        values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
      });
    }

    throw exception('REFUND_WINDOW_EXPIRED');
  }
}

@start errors.json
{
  "ORDER_NOT_FOUND": { "statusCode": 404, "sentry": false },
  "ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED": { "statusCode": 409, "sentry": false },
  "CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND": { "statusCode": 404, "sentry": false },
  "REFUND_FAILED": { "statusCode": 502, "sentry": true }
}

@expected errors.json
{
  "ORDER_NOT_FOUND": { "statusCode": 404, "sentry": false },
  "ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED": { "statusCode": 409, "sentry": false },
  "REFUND_WINDOW_EXPIRED": { "statusCode": 409, "sentry": false },
  "CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND": { "statusCode": 404, "sentry": false },
  "REFUND_FAILED": { "statusCode": 502, "sentry": true }
}
